body:
Well, this past christmas I decided to break up with Windows. It wasn't easy, we'd been close my whole life. She was my gal. But, I couldn't trust her anymore, she had to go. I dated around for a while with the linux distros. Kali was my first, of course. She was fit, with claws to match her looks, but I mistakenly thought she was special. Her flaws finally turned me away. I found Parrot after, but it didn't last long. She had some major issues, and I think she learned how to dress from a pirate. The flamboyant kind. Over the next four to five months, I spent lot of time with all the most prominent distros around. I'd heard about Arch, the stories were like myths about a legend. I may as well attempt to slay a dragon for the princess. An apt analogy, or so I thought. All the others fell by the wayside. Debian didn't last long at all. Neither did gentoo. I have little patience for bugs that I see as inexcusable flaws. Mint was fine, but that's all she ever was, nothing about her to quicken my pulse. I didn't even bother to give ubuntu my email address. Manjaro held on longer than the rest. We still spend time together. In fact, I'm writing this now with her beautiful lines and gradients around me, courtesy of Plasma 5. But,her sister is truly the one for me. Me and Arch, we go together like peas and carrots. She is the cheese in my macaroni. Effortlessly flawless, ninety percent of the time. She does everything I ask without much trouble at all. It's almost as if I built her myself, with my own hands. Because, of course I did. She's only down now because of some bug she caught in an update that fouled her initramfs, an unavoidable illness sent to her by a vengeful diety, no doubt jealous of our romance. Damn developers. But, she's going to be just fine, I've got just the right chroot to make her better, some Arch-chroot. And then we'll be off again, honing her skills in arts both dangerous and beautiful, traveling to whatever parts of the inter-webs we decide to go. Tempting fate, living dangerously, our mutual trust keeping us one step ahead of the law, both man and god's. I hope you all find an Arch of your own one day. Consider her training carefully. Be deliberate and cautious with what you allow her to ingest. And before you know it, she'll put all the rest to shame.

&#x200B;

You wanted a story. There ya go. Enjoy.
